B1 — BLOCKING. `done` with no recorded red for any of twelve behaviours.

  studio/ci/reds/W-021/ does not exist. The brief lists twelve behaviours and
  requires a red for each, recorded with `bisellium red` before the behaviour
  is implemented. Engineering lex §7 makes that a condition of `done`:
  "Every `done` item carries: test log path, review note path, and the
  recorded red for every behaviour its spec listed." CLAUDE.md's standing
  rule is the same in one line.

  What exists instead is ~/.bisellium-evidence/cascade-6/w021/, outside the
  officina:
    run1-red.txt    ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND, exit 1 — the implementation file
                    absent from the worktree. One red for the whole test
                    file. It does not discriminate behaviour 1 from
                    behaviour 12 and shows no assertion failing.
    run2.txt        all green except behaviour 12, which failed at `block`
                    on the acta's ../README.md citation.
    run3-green.txt  green after the level drop to `advise`.

  I credit run2.txt: it is a real, dated red for behaviour 12 and it is what
  drove the insurance clause. That is one behaviour out of twelve, and it is
  still not in the officina.

  This opus wrote the rule that blocks exactly this shape. `opus.red_evidence`
  is silent on W-021 only because the opus was advanced to `done`, which takes
  it out of the rule's building|verifying|review scope. Advancing the opus is
  what silenced its own rule. I confirmed the rule bites correctly the moment
  the state is active.

  The root cause is not builder-b's diligence. The brief required W-020 to
  land `bisellium red` on master as its first commit so builder-b could record
  into the store; the harness ran both builders in parallel worktrees off one
  base, so that commit never existed for builder-b to use. Builder-b captured
  a genuine red and kept it outside the officina rather than forge one. That
  is the right instinct.

  It is also not now fixable. A red recorded today, against a tree where the
  implementation exists, is backfill — "evidence is produced, never
  backfilled" — and `bisellium red` refuses it by design. The evidence is
  permanently unavailable for this opus.

  So: the work passes review, the close does not. The remedy is the Patron's,
  not mine — waive §7 for W-021 against the recorded orchestration root cause,
  or reopen the opus. I will not pass a `done` that the officina's own rule
  would block, on a gap this opus exists to make unforgivable.


F2 — non-blocking. A passing run dressed as a red gets through.

  packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.ts:191

      if (exit === undefined || !/^-?\d+$/.test(exit) || exit === "0") ...

  `exit === "0"` is a string comparison. `# exit: 00` and `# exit: -0` both
  satisfy the integer regex and are both unequal to "0", so a log recording a
  passing run reads as a genuine red. Confirmed against the built module:

      # exit: 0     → blocks      (correct)
      # exit: +0    → blocks      (correct — fails the regex)
      # exit: 00    → NO FINDING  (wrong)

  Behaviour 7's contract is the header being `0`, and `00` is `0`. One
  character: `Number(exit) === 0`. Not blocking — it needs a hand-forged log,
  which is already forbidden, and no log in either officina has this shape.


F3 — non-blocking, scope. `opus.untracked` is blind to a gitignored opus.

  `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` omits ignored files, so an opus
  matched by one .gitignore line reads as clean. Confirmed on a throwaway
  repo: an active opus at studio/opera/W-901.md, gitignored, produces no
  finding. The rule's stated purpose is that work must be attributable; a
  one-line .gitignore defeats it silently.

  The brief prescribed that exact command and those exact untracked-only
  semantics, so this is not a defect against spec. It belongs in the retro
  or a petitio as a scope question for the rule's next revision.


F4 — non-blocking here, but it is a false-certificate mechanism.

  packages/shim/src/redact.ts:25-26

      const ALLOWLIST = /^(PATH|HOME|NODE_.*|LANG|TZ)$/;
      const SECRET_NAME = /TOKEN|SECRET|KEY|PASSWORD|CREDENTIAL/i;

  `filterEnv` drops GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0 and GIT_CONFIG_KEY_1 — the name matches
  /KEY/i and is not on the allowlist — while keeping GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=2 and
  the matching VALUE_n. Git then refuses every invocation:

      error: missing config key GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0
      fatal: unable to parse command-line config          (exit 128)

  Every git call inside a command `bisellium verify` runs dies. Proven on one
  tree, one commit, three runs:

      bisellium verify W-021 …                      tests: failed
      bisellium verify W-021 … (GIT_CONFIG_* unset) tests: passed
      npm test, standalone                          exit 0, 859 PASS, 0 FAIL

  The failure surfaced at packages/cli/src/test.ts:121 → sourceTreeHash →
  `git ls-tree -r HEAD`. This is what produced round 1's false tests-red as
  well. Not W-021's file — it is shim/pipeline, W-008/W-009 — and not this
  opus's to fix, but a probatio pipeline that mints a red the tree does not
  deserve is the exact failure class the officina exists to prevent. It needs
  its own opus. The narrow fix is to allowlist GIT_CONFIG_KEY_\d+ or to drop
  GIT_CONFIG_COUNT and every GIT_CONFIG_* together rather than half of them.


F5 — accepted. Behaviour 12's assertion over `studio` was relaxed.

  packages/cli/src/rules.test.ts:379-380 asserts no *blocking* path.* finding
  over `studio`, where the brief's behaviour 12 asked for no path.* finding at
  all. That follows necessarily from the level drop; the literal assertion is
  unsatisfiable once the rule advises. It is documented in the test comment
  and in P-004, and run2.txt shows the original assertion genuinely red before
  it was changed. Correct handling.


F6 — noted. The clause was applied to both path.* rules.

  Only `path.escapes.officina` ever fired; `path.id.unvalidated` is clean on
  both officinae. Both dropped to `advise`. The brief's acceptance says "drop
  the two path.* rules to advise" verbatim, so this is compliant — recorded
